---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
---
<Layout
  title="The Missing Guarantee — Shaurya Vijay Sinha"
  description="A proposed AI and Deep-Tech Survival Bridge Guarantee (ADSBG) for India, stress-tested against a Year-1 fiscal exposure model."
>
  <article class="section section--tight ghost-figure-host reveal">
    <span class="ghost-figure" aria-hidden="true">195 Cr</span>
    <div class="wrap prose">
      <p class="eyebrow">Credit-guarantee design · An independent analysis following Girish Kuber's <em>The Tatas</em></p>
      <h1>The Missing Guarantee</h1>
      <p style="font-size:1.05rem; font-style:italic; color:var(--body-text); margin-top:-0.4em;">
        A credit instrument for India's AI and deep-tech sector, argued against
        Girish Kuber's <em>The Tatas: How a Family Built a Business and a Nation</em>.
      </p>

      <div class="key-finding key-finding--with-chart">
        <div>
          <p class="key-finding__label">Key finding, from the accompanying feasibility model</p>
          <p class="key-finding__figure">Rs 195 Cr Year-1 shortfall</p>
          <p class="key-finding__context">
            At base-case assumptions — 200 loans, an average ticket of Rs 15 crore,
            75% guarantee cover, a 2.5% annual fee, and a 15% default rate — the
            scheme collects Rs 75 crore in premium against an expected claim payout
            of Rs 270 crore, net of recovery. The fee alone does not cover Year-1
            losses; either the fee rises, or the government accepts a subsidised
            early phase, the way CGSS's champion-sector fee cut already does.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <svg class="mini-chart" viewBox="0 0 220 150" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" role="img" aria-label="Bar chart comparing Year-1 premium income of Rs 75 crore against expected claim payout of Rs 270 crore">
            <line x1="20" y1="120" x2="200" y2="120" stroke="var(--rule-faint)" stroke-width="1" />
            <rect x="55" y="92" width="42" height="28" fill="var(--rule)" />
            <text x="76" y="84" text-anchor="middle">₹75 Cr</text>
            <text x="76" y="136" text-anchor="middle">Premium</text>
            <rect x="135" y="20" width="42" height="100" fill="var(--navy)" />
            <text x="156" y="14" text-anchor="middle">₹270 Cr</text>
            <text x="156" y="136" text-anchor="middle">Payout</text>
          </svg>
          <p class="mini-chart__caption">Year-1 premium income vs. expected claim payout, net of recovery</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <h2>The parallel</h2>
      <p>
        In 1906–07, Sir Dorabji Tata went to London to raise capital for what
        became Tata Iron and Steel. British bankers turned him down — an
        unproven Indian steel venture was not a risk they were willing to
        price. What actually funded TISCO was a public share issue, absorbed by
        Indian retail investors on swadeshi sentiment rather than institutional
        confidence, and the company ran at a loss for years before wartime
        steel demand made the bet pay off.
      </p>
      <p>
        Strip away the nationalism and it's a capital-markets story: a
        technically unproven, capital-intensive project with a long wait before
        any payoff, funded only because informal capital stepped in where
        formal lenders wouldn't. That is close to the shape of an Indian AI or
        deep-tech startup today — and the resemblance is not a coincidence so
        much as a pattern that recurs whenever India tries to build frontier
        industry with no precedent to underwrite against.
      </p>

      <h2>Why the existing schemes don't fit</h2>
      <p>
        India already runs credit guarantees purpose-built for specific gaps:
        CGTMSE for MSMEs, CGSS for DPIIT-recognised startups, CGFMU for
        first-generation entrepreneurs via Mudra loans, ECGC for exporters,
        NABARD's equivalent for agriculture. None of them fit AI and deep-tech,
        because none were priced for this risk profile. CGTMSE assumes a
        borrower with predictable revenue inside a year or two; an AI company
        with no revenue for three to five years is asking a lender to judge
        whether unproven technology will eventually work, not whether a
        business will repay on schedule. Ordinary credit underwriting answers
        the second question. It has no tools for the first.
      </p>

      <h2>The proposal</h2>
      <p>
        An AI and Deep-Tech Survival Bridge Guarantee (ADSBG) would work on the
        same logic as CGTMSE and CGSS: government does not fund companies
        directly, it provides a partial loss guarantee funded by a small annual
        premium, so private lenders and investors take on risk they would
        otherwise avoid. The design difference is eligibility: instead of
        revenue history, ADSBG would gate on independently certified Technology
        Readiness Levels, verified by a Technical Evaluation Committee drawn
        from the IITs, IISc, and MeitY — decoupling technical assessment from
        credit risk, so lenders underwrite financial execution and not the
        underlying science. Because pre-revenue deep-tech firms can't service
        standard monthly principal and interest, the guarantee would sit
        specifically around venture debt, convertible notes, or loans carrying
        extended moratoriums of three to five years.
      </p>

      <div class="exhibit">
        <img src="/images/adsbg-chart-2.png" alt="Line chart comparing ADSBG's projected cumulative exposure against CGSS's actual growth trajectory over ten years, both starting near zero and ADSBG rising faster." loading="lazy" />
        <figcaption>Exhibit — ADSBG's projected exposure modelled on CGSS's early trajectory (40% annual growth from a Year-1 base of Rs 2,250 crore), not CGTMSE's mature, decades-old scale.</figcaption>
      </div>

      <h2>What the model is actually measuring</h2>
      <p>
        The Year-1 shortfall isn't an argument against the scheme — CGTMSE and
        CGSS aren't designed to turn a profit either; the premiums collected
        only aim to cover expected losses. The real return is second-order:
        companies that survive the early funding gap go on to create jobs, pay
        taxes, and attract further private capital. There's no clean study
        proving how much CGTMSE alone contributed to the MSME sector's scale,
        and ADSBG's success shouldn't be judged by revenue either — only by how
        many companies it helps survive that would otherwise have failed.
      </p>
      <p>
        The scale of the gap is what makes the case. India's cumulative
        private AI investment sits at roughly $11.1 billion against $470.9
        billion in the US and $119.3 billion in China over the same window —
        2.4% and 9.3% of those figures respectively. That gap, not ambition, is
        what ADSBG is built to close.
      </p>
    </div>
  </article>

  <section class="section--tight reveal">
    <div class="wrap" style="display:flex; gap:var(--space-3); flex-wrap:wrap; align-items:center; justify-content:space-between; border-top:1px solid var(--rule); padding-top:var(--space-3);">
      <p style="margin:0; max-width:32rem;">Download the feasibility model — benchmark data, the Year-1 exposure calculator, a 10-year growth projection, and the funding-gap sourcing, all with linked citations.</p>
      <a class="btn btn--solid" href="/files/ADSBG_Feasibility_Model.xlsx" download>Download the model (.xlsx)</a>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section--tight">
    <div class="wrap">
      <a href="/research/">← Back to Research</a>
    </div>
  </section>
</Layout>
